      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.476,
            0.326,
            0.566,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": "Octahedron"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.581,
            0.048,
            0.861,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Exploring Some Geometric Themes"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.089,
            0.847,
            0.129
          ],
          "content": "Here is a solid called the **octahedron**, made by joining two square pyramids at their square bases."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.391,
            0.139,
            0.63,
            0.319
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.357,
            0.634,
            0.377
          ],
          "content": "Can you visualise its net? This is one of its nets."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.391,
            0.384,
            0.63,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.535,
            0.787,
            0.575
          ],
          "content": "？ Taking all the triangles in the net to be equilateral, make a cutout of the net and fold it to form an octahedron."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.801,
            0.526,
            0.889,
            0.585
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.592,
            0.767,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": "As in the case of cubes, an octahedron too has 11 different nets."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.61,
            0.846,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": "So far, we have seen solids whose faces are all equilateral triangles (regular tetrahedrons), and all squares (cubes). Does there exist a solid whose faces are all pentagons? Interestingly, the answer is yes!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.665,
            0.847,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": "This solid is called a **dodecahedron**. This solid too can be made from a net! Mathematicians have been even able to determine exactly how many nets a dodecahedron has — 43,380."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.419,
            0.736,
            0.627,
            0.89
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.468,
                0.896,
                0.576,
                0.91
              ],
              "content": "Dodecahedron"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
